; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What every tool_sort variant shares, stated once.
;
; Eleven documents stand in one workcell, on one robot, under one import
; profile. Everything a loader used to add after lowering lives here now:
; the suite's description and provenance, `(:arms 1)` and `(:monolithic
; true)` for the requirements, the predicate table the documents are read
; under, the collider every free tool wears, the order the variants are
; listed in, and the three foreign pairings that stand another robot in the
; cell -- and the one look all eleven render under. The workcell static, the
; sidecar's start poses and the welded container's region rebase need no
; clause at all: `(:export X)` in each document is what the library acts on,
; and `(:objects-from ...)` on the two USD restatements is acted on the same
; way.
;
; What is NOT here, and why. The documents each keep their own `(:profile
; ...)`, their `bin1`/`bin2` fixtures and their own `:artifacts` entries --
; which merge with the suite's by kind, so the `:render` below reaches all
; eleven while each keeps its routine, its tools and its graphs -- although
; ten of the eleven agree on them: a document is read on its own by the
; exporter, the typechecker sweep and the lowering tests, and a document that
; only made sense beside this file would be a document that could not be
; handed to somebody. The suite restates the profile so a reader of this file
; alone knows which convention the variants inherit; `syringe` overrides it
; with `vab_industrial@v1`, a sibling profile differing in one camera.
;
; The suite's provenance is prose written as bare tokens, which the reader
; joins back into one sentence; `;` opens a comment and a parenthesis opens a
; form, which is why the sentence has neither. A "..." string would do as
; well now that the reader has one -- the `:notes` on the hosts below use it.
; -------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (suite tool_sort)

  (:language Sort YCB hand tools out of a cluttered bin into labelled
             compartments, on a Franka Panda)

  ; Headline first: a bare `tool_sort` resolves to `sort_all`, and every
  ; golden, test and tool has always said `tool_sort/sort_all`. The names are
  ; what each variant IS rather than what its problem asks -- `hammer` is
  ; PICK_UP_THE_HAMMER_AND_PLACE_IT_IN_BIN2's document, and the problem name
  ; inside each file is still the upstream one.
  ;
  ; `syringe` is last rather than tenth because it left and came back. It was
  ; its own suite, `sharps_disposal`, on the argument that posting one object
  ; through a sealed aperture is a different skill from sorting a pile into
  ; compartments -- which is true, and `sort_power`'s third goal is that same
  ; skill inside this suite anyway. The fork was 59% byte-identical to this
  ; directory and its tools.py differed in four lines, so it merged back on
  ; 2026-09-02 (as the five bench suites merged into `rgbs`). Its header says
  ; what it kept of its own.
  ;
  ; `level1_N` is the thirty-document Level-1 tier, delivered September 2026:
  ; four separated industrial objects per scene, each with a labelled
  ; compartment of its own, staged under `vab_industrial_robotiq@v1` rather
  ; than this suite's own profile. They are listed after the eleven above
  ; rather than among them because the headline has to stay `sort_all`, and
  ; because the tier is a sweep -- one draw per document -- rather than eleven
  ; scenes each worth an argument.
  ;
  ; `level2_N` is the heap tier above it, the same way: the same bench and pool,
  ; but 8 to 12 objects dropped into a pile rather than laid out, so an object
  ; can be under another. Ported from RoboSimStudio-dev `tool_perception`
  ; (7ffc4017, 97309ce3, 067645c6).
  (:variants sort_all sort_all_neat hammer sort_new sort_bench sort_power
             sort_electrical sort_wiring sort_electrical_usd sort_electrical_usd_geom
             syringe
             level1_1 level1_2 level1_3 level1_4 level1_5 level1_6
             level1_7 level1_8 level1_9 level1_10 level1_11 level1_12
             level1_13 level1_14 level1_15 level1_16 level1_17 level1_18
             level1_19 level1_20 level1_21 level1_22 level1_23 level1_24
             level1_25 level1_26 level1_27 level1_28 level1_29 level1_30
             level2_1 level2_2 level2_3 level2_4 level2_5 level2_6
             level2_7 level2_8 level2_9 level2_10 level2_11 level2_12
             level2_13 level2_14 level2_15 level2_16 level2_17 level2_18
             level2_19 level2_20 level2_21 level2_22 level2_23 level2_24
             level2_25 level2_26 level2_27 level2_28 level2_29 level2_30)

  (:staging
    (:profile vab_tool_sort@v2)

    ; One arm. Every variant is a one-handed pick-and-place on the Panda the
    ; export welds into the cell.
    (:arms 1)

    ; The arena and its robot arrive in one import, so neither axis can be
    ; swapped until a binding names the robot-stripped variant -- which is
    ; what every `:host` below does -- and the profile's `scene-resident` is
    ; the other half of that, without which the compiler would import the
    ; extracted standalone Panda as well and the scene would hold two arms.
    (:monolithic true)

    ; Which predicate semantics these files are read under.
    ;
    ; Reproduction tier: `In` binds to the weak box test the source benchmark
    ; uses, not to the stronger contact-and-at-rest predicate this stack also
    ; has. That is a deliberate choice for comparability and is recorded in
    ; every built spec's provenance.
    ;

    ; What a free object in these cells collides on, and why it is not the box.
    ;
    ; The export fits each tool one oriented box and marks the mesh beside it
    ; `contype="0" conaffinity="0"` -- drawn, never touched. That box is VAB's
    ; own approximation, not this repository's, and it is a big one. Measured
    ; as the largest distance, over every heading a top-down jaw could close
    ; along, that the collider stands proud of the mesh a viewer sees:
    ;
    ;     object                 authored box     drawn hull
    ;     drill                     27.1 mm         0.00 mm
    ;     clamp                     26.2            0.00
    ;     adjustable_wrench         19.5            0.00
    ;     pliers                    16.8            0.00
    ;     utility_knife             15.9            0.00
    ;     phillips_screwdriver       8.9            0.00
    ;
    ; Two centimetres of invisible shell is a gripper that closes on nothing
    ; and a Robotiq linkage that deflects against it, which is what this suite
    ; looked like.
    ;
    ; THIS IS A DEPARTURE FROM THE EXPORT AND IT IS NARROW. The hammer and the
    ; round nut come through untouched, because upstream authored *them* as
    ; real primitives -- a cylinder handle, a box head, nine boxes for the nut
    ; -- and those already measure 0.00 mm proud. What `drawn_hull` replaces
    ; is only the case where the file itself says "this shape is for looking
    ; at": see robosimstudio.domains.rigid.EntityRigid._add_drawn_collider.
    ;
    ; The old note here said boxes were chosen because "a raw convex hull
    ; leaves a thin tool rocking in a limit cycle that creeps 15-25 mm while
    ; nominally settled". That was true and the cause was elsewhere:
    ; mujoco_warp gives a convex pair one contact point unless
    ; `enable_multiccd` is on, and one point is what a body rocks about.
    ; `vab_500hz@v1` states the flag; without it the same creep comes straight
    ; back.
    ;
    ; The three `sort_electrical*` documents override this with the box the
    ; USD stage authors: they are one scene described three ways, and the
    ; collider is one of the things held still. See sort_electrical.task.
    (:collider drawn_hull)
  )

  ; The pairings that stand a foreign robot in this workcell.
  ;
  ; Each carries a `static-override` naming a *different file* -- the same
  ; arena with the Panda cut out of it. The export welds its robot into the
  ; room, so a foreign one needs the *stripped* file, and a bare file name
  ; resolves under each variant's own `(:export ...)`: four of the six exports
  ; carry their own object set, so which file it is depends on the variant.
  ; `scene-resident` is left unstated (false) because the point is that there
  ; is no longer a robot in there to index.
  ;
  ; What a document must never do is assert that a foreign pairing *works*.
  ; That is a fact about the matrix, discovered by running it, and a file
  ; asserting it would go stale with nothing to notice. These rows say only
  ; "here is how to stand this robot here", and every one carries the note it
  ; has always had.
  (:hosts
    ; The stripped arena hosting a YAM.
    (:host (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left))
           (:static-override workcell scene_norobot.xml)
           (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
    ; The same stripped arena hosting the six-jointed arm. Its hand is welded
    ; on rather than shipped with it, so this row is the whole fourth axis in
    ; one binding: a task's scene, another robot, and a hand that came from a
    ; third file.
    (:host (:embodiment ur5 (:work-arm right))
           (:static-override workcell scene_norobot.xml)
           (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
    ; The same robot wearing a different hand -- the fourth axis, taking a
    ; value. The i2rt gripper was measured on a YAM and its closing axis
    ; holds to 0.3 degrees here, which is what makes it one hand rather than
    ; two that happen to share a name.
    (:host (:embodiment ur5 (:work-arm right)) (:gripper linear_4310_attached)
           (:static-override workcell scene_norobot.xml)
           (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
    ; The export's own Panda wearing a Robotiq 2F-85 is NOT a row here, and the
    ; Level-1 delivery's attempt to add one is why this note exists. A `:host`
    ; is a pairing offered to every variant in the suite, and the thirty
    ; `level1_N` documents already stage that exact pairing themselves through
    ; `vab_industrial_robotiq@v1` -- so the row lands a second time on each of
    ; them and the suite refuses to build: "duplicate binding for
    ; scene='robosuite_bench' embodiment='panda_robotiq' gripper=''".
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; How the cell looks under the path tracer: the wood, steel and print the
  ; export's own MJCF states, and a lit shop around it. Every variant renders
  ; under it. The materials themselves are put on the shapes by the workcell
  ; entity each document names -- see assets/look.py for why one clause here
  ; could not have done it, and for the two places a texture was being lost.
  (:artifacts
    (:render "assets.look:TOOL_SORT_LOOK")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:source VAB-industrial ToolSortPickPlace,
             robosuite 1.4.0, MuJoCo 3.3.3, seed 0 -- regions and goals
             transcribed into the .task documents beside this file, see any of
             their headers for what 'reconstructed' means and why a round trip
             through them proves less than it looks)
  )
)
